; pi4MmuIdentity.pi4 - STAGE 1. Build identity page tables, dump them,
;                      turn the MMU on, prove the board still works,
;                      turn it off again and hand the monitor back.
;
; DIAGNOSTIC. Does not ship.
;
;   pmfc pi4MmuIdentity.pi4 -t pi4 --load-addr 0x400000 \
;        --stack-addr 0x3000000 --entry-returns -o mmuid.img
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHAT THIS IS FOR
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; Stage 1's goal is a NON-EVENT. Nothing is cached before and nothing is
; cached after; every address means what it meant; every driver's DMA
; coherency is untouched. If the console is still alive on the far side
; of the enabling instruction, then the descriptor format, the TCR and
; MAIR programming, the table walk and the enable sequence are all
; correct - and stages 2, 3 and 4 become changes of attribute bits on a
; mechanism that has been proven rather than a leap.
;
; See RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4's STAGE 1 section for the map, the
; geometry and the citation behind every constant.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE ORDER OF OPERATIONS, WHICH IS THE ENTIRE SAFETY DESIGN
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; A hang with the MMU half-enabled gives no console and no clue. So
; EVERYTHING THAT CAN BE CHECKED IS CHECKED AND PRINTED BEFORE THE
; SWITCH, and the switch is the last thing that happens:
;
;   1. print the pre-state, so a failure later has a baseline
;   2. build the tables - this only writes DRAM and is perfectly safe
;   3. DUMP THE TABLES and let a human read them against the map
;   4. re-arm the deadman, so the full ~15.999 s covers the switch
;   5. enable
;   6. print a marker - if this appears, the map is right
;   7. read SCTLR_EL2 back and confirm bit 0 is now set
;   8. disable
;   9. print the post-state and return to the monitor, MMU off
;
; Step 3 is the one that earns its place. The table builder is ordinary
; code writing ordinary memory, and its output is completely inspectable
; with the MMU still off. Every bug that would hang the board at step 5
; is visible at step 3 to anyone willing to read sixteen hex digits.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHERE THE TABLES GO, AND WHY THERE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; $00600000 - six megabytes, inside the monitor's LOW payload window
; (up to $07EFFFFF; its base is above the monitor and `map` prints it),
; clear of this payload's own image wherever it was staged, and far below
; the payload stack top this is built with. It is 2 MiB aligned and
; therefore certainly 4 KiB
; aligned, which MmuBuildTables() requires and checks.
;
; It is a constant here rather than inside the library on purpose: where
; the tables live is a POLICY decision belonging to whoever owns the
; memory map, and a library that hard-coded it would be wrong the first
; time somebody wanted them somewhere else.
#TABLES = $00600000

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE BENCH PROCEDURE, AND WHAT EACH OUTCOME MEANS
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;   pmf> deadman 15
;   pmf> map              <- it prints `stage a file at <stage>`. The
;                            --load-addr above does not pin the image.
;   pmf> receive <stage> <len> <crc>
;   pmf> run <stage>
;
; ARM THE DEADMAN. It is the difference between a fifteen-second delay
; and somebody walking to the bench. The monitor's deadman covers the
; whole payload including the switch; this probe ALSO re-arms it
; immediately before the enable so that the full ~15.999 s ceiling is
; ahead of the dangerous instruction rather than partly spent on the
; table dump. Both are the same PM watchdog, and it is already counting
; before the MMU changes, so a bad map cannot stop it firing.
;
; OUTCOME A - everything prints, "MMU OFF AGAIN" appears, prompt returns.
;   STAGE 1 PASSES. The tables, the attributes and the enable sequence
;   are all correct. Report the SCTLR_EL2 values from steps 1, 7 and 9.
;
; OUTCOME B - output stops exactly after "ENABLING NOW".
;   The map or the enable sequence is wrong. This is the expected shape
;   of a stage 1 failure and it is not alarming: the deadman fires
;   within ~16 s, the board resets, the firmware reloads kernel8.img,
;   and Anvil comes back with the MMU off because the stub enters it
;   that way. NOTHING TO REVERT - this is a payload; anvil.img was never
;   touched. Report the last line printed and the table dump above it.
;
; OUTCOME C - output stops during the table dump, before "ENABLING NOW".
;   Nothing to do with the MMU. That is ordinary code writing ordinary
;   DRAM with the MMU still off, so it is a UART or transfer problem,
;   or #TABLES is somewhere it should not be.
;
; OUTCOME D - "THIS BUILD CANNOT ENABLE" appears and the probe stops
;   before the switch. Not a hardware result. The image was built while
;   #MMU_HAVE_EL2_REGS was still 0, i.e. before the A64 assembler
;   learned TTBR0_EL2, TCR_EL2 and MAIR_EL2. Rebuild once it has.
;
; OUTCOME E - the board does not come back at all after ~20 s.
;   The deadman did not fire. POWER-CYCLE IT. That is the full cost -
;   the board is not damaged and nothing needs reverting or reflashing.
;   Worth reporting, because a deadman that failed to fire is a separate
;   and more interesting bug than a bad page table.
;
; WHAT WOULD TELL YOU IT WENT SUBTLY WRONG RATHER THAN LOUDLY WRONG:
; the probe completes but SCTLR_EL2 at step 7 does NOT have bit 0 set.
; That means the enable sequence ran and the MMU did not come on, which
; is worse than a hang because it looks like a pass. The probe checks
; this itself and says so rather than leaving it to the reader.
;
; AND ONE THING THAT IS NOT A FAULT: the console may get FASTER. DRAM is
; Normal Non-Cacheable after the switch rather than Device, so writes
; can be gathered. That is a correct result. See ONE DELIBERATE
; DEPARTURE in RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4 for why DRAM is Normal NC and
; not Device.

XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/uart.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/safety.pi4"
XIncludeFile "RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4"

Procedure PutHex16(v.i)
  Define i.i
  Define n.i
  For i = 15 To 0 Step -1
    n = (v >> (i * 4)) & $F
    If n < 10
      UartWrite(48 + n)
    Else
      UartWrite(55 + n)
    EndIf
  Next
EndProcedure

Procedure Row(*label, v.i)
  UartWriteStr(*label)
  UartWrite(36)                 ; the dollar sign that marks hex here
  PutHex16(v)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

Procedure Say(*s)
  UartWriteStr(*s)
  UartWriteNl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  DumpTables - the whole point of step 3.
;
;  Prints every level-1 slot that is not a fault, and the three level-2
;  slots that matter: the last DRAM slot, the first peripheral slot, and
;  the slot holding the ARM-local window. Printing all 512 level-2 slots
;  would be minutes of console and would bury the four numbers that
;  actually decide whether this works.
;
;  READ THESE AGAINST THE MAP in RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4. What each
;  should be:
;
;    L1 slot 0   $0000000000000<70D>   Normal NC at PA 0
;    L1 slot 1   $000000004000070D     Normal NC at PA $40000000
;    L1 slot 2   $000000008000070D     Normal NC at PA $80000000
;    L1 slot 3   $0000000000601003     TABLE, pointing at #TABLES+4096
;    L1 slot 24  $0000000600000401     Device at PA $600000000
;    L1 slot 31  $00000007C0000401     Device at PA $7C0000000
;    L2 slot 479 $00000000FBE0070D     Normal NC, the top of DRAM
;    L2 slot 480 $00000000FC000401     Device, the peripheral aperture
;    L2 slot 508 $00000000FF800401     Device, the ARM-local window
;
;  THE TWO THAT MATTER MOST are L1 slot 24 - if that is zero, USB dies
;  the moment anything touches the VL805 - and L2 slot 508, because
;  without it core 1 can never be released.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure DumpTables()
  Define i.i

  Say("level 1, the slots that are mapped:")
  For i = 0 To #MMU_L1_ENTRIES - 1
    If MmuDescriptor(#TABLES, i) <> 0
      UartWriteStr("  L1 slot ")
      PrintDec(i)
      UartWriteStr("  ")
      UartWrite(36)
      PutHex16(MmuDescriptor(#TABLES, i))
      UartWriteNl()
    EndIf
  Next

  Say("level 2, the three boundaries that decide it:")
  Row("  L2 slot 479 top of DRAM   ", MmuDescriptorL2(#TABLES, 479))
  Row("  L2 slot 480 peripherals   ", MmuDescriptorL2(#TABLES, 480))
  Row("  L2 slot 508 ARM-local     ", MmuDescriptorL2(#TABLES, 508))
EndProcedure

Procedure.i Main()
  Define rc.i
  Define before.i
  Define during.i
  Define after.i

  UartInit(115200)
  SafetyWatchdogStart(15)

  UartWriteNl()
  Say("pi4MmuIdentity - stage 1, identity map, nothing cached")
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ---- 1. the pre-state --------------------------------------------
  before = MmuSctlrEl2()
  Row("SCTLR_EL2 before  ", before)
  Row("exception level   ", MmuEl())
  Row("MMU on before     ", MmuEnabled())
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ---- 2. build ------------------------------------------------------
  Row("tables at         ", #TABLES)
  rc = MmuBuildTables(#TABLES)
  Row("build returned    ", rc)
  If rc <> 0
    Say("REFUSED. See #MMU_ERR_* in RaspberryPi4/Lib/mmu.pi4.")
    Say("Nothing was enabled. The board is untouched.")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn rc
  EndIf
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ---- 3. dump -------------------------------------------------------
  DumpTables()
  UartWriteNl()

  ; ---- 4. can this build even do it? --------------------------------
  If MmuBuildable() = 0
    Say("THIS BUILD CANNOT ENABLE - #MMU_HAVE_EL2_REGS is 0, so the")
    Say("A64 assembler had no encoding for TTBR0_EL2 / TCR_EL2 /")
    Say("MAIR_EL2 when this image was built. The tables above are")
    Say("real and were checked; the switch was NOT attempted.")
    Say("Rebuild once the assembler has them. Outcome D.")
    UartDrain()
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  ; ---- 5. the switch -------------------------------------------------
  ; Re-arm so the whole ceiling is ahead of the dangerous instruction
  ; rather than partly spent printing the dump above.
  SafetyWatchdogStart(15)

  Say("ENABLING NOW - if this is the last line you see, outcome B:")
  Say("the deadman resets the board in ~16 s and Anvil returns with")
  Say("the MMU off. Nothing to revert.")
  UartDrain()

  MmuEnable()

  ; ---- 6. we are alive on the other side ----------------------------
  Say("ALIVE - the console works with the MMU ON.")
  during = MmuSctlrEl2()
  Row("SCTLR_EL2 during  ", during)
  Row("MMU on during     ", MmuEnabled())

  ; ---- 7. the silent-failure check ----------------------------------
  If MmuEnabled() = 0
    Say("WRONG: the enable sequence ran and bit 0 is still clear.")
    Say("The MMU did NOT come on. This looks like a pass and is not.")
  EndIf
  UartWriteNl()
  UartDrain()

  ; ---- 8. put it back ------------------------------------------------
  MmuDisable()

  ; ---- 9. the post-state ---------------------------------------------
  after = MmuSctlrEl2()
  Row("SCTLR_EL2 after   ", after)
  Row("MMU on after      ", MmuEnabled())
  If after = before
    Say("MMU OFF AGAIN - SCTLR_EL2 is byte for byte what it was.")
    Say("STAGE 1 PASSES. Outcome A.")
  Else
    Say("MMU off, but SCTLR_EL2 did not return to its entry value.")
    Say("Report both numbers - something other than bit 0 moved.")
  EndIf

  UartDrain()
  ProcedureReturn 0
EndProcedure
